WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.259 --> 00:00:31.000
[Music]

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:35.459
[Narrator:] Suppose you're around a field kitchen when hot water spills over the cook and burns him.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:41.992
Or suppose you're around when a member of a wire-laying crew falls from a tree and breaks his leg.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:47.292
Or suppose you see an Army truck smash-up and several men are severely injured.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:50.560
You don't have to be a doctor to help.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:56.960
In fact, as a first-aider, you are not expected to do a doctor's job, or to use complicated medical equipment.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:03.459
What will you do for a man with a broken back? Or a severe burn? Or a broken leg?

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.559
Would you get panicky and run around in circles because a doctor wasn't around?

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:12.492
You wouldn't if you knew something about first aid.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:19.092
A first-aider knows what help to give an injured person before a doctor's treatment or equipment is available.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:25.620
He first determines what is wrong with the injured person, and then acts quickly, but gently.

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:32.520
And accidents will happen. Snakebites, broken bones, electric shock, burns.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:38.980
Such non-battle injuries can cause loss of life or limb, if you don't know something about first aid.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.320
Sometimes, accidents are just around the corner.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:46.380
This truck, coming down the road, was six miles from camp.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:48.620
Men were in the front and in the rear.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.740
The truck was about to make a curve, when a road hog forced the truck to swerve to a soft shoulder.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:57.780
[Car crash sounds ]

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:06.092
A corporal [?] real injury and knew enough about first aid to take control of the situation.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.280
His first move was to send for an ambulance.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:14.300
Then, he started to look after the injured.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:24.120
The driver had been thrown out of the cab, and it looked like curtains for him, but the corporal started right in with first aid.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:34.440
He first examined the driver, and figured he had a broken back, because when he tried to move him, even a little bit, it hurt like hell.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:47.720
The corporal didn't pick up the driver, because he knew a fella with a broken back should never be moved except by skilled medical men.

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:51.020
He was smart enough not to do too much.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:59.460
He took a couple of field jackets, rolled them up, 
and gently placed them under the small of the back

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:05.080
by rolling the driver to one side while the jackets were being placed.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:11.380
This gave support to the back so that the man was more comfortable.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.859
He reassured the driver that medical help was on the way,

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:18.220
and then went to check on the condition of the others.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:23.700
These men were pretty well banged up.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:29.140
This one had a bad flesh wound on the thigh.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:42.220
The blood was oozing, but not spurting.

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:56.780
So, the corporal gave the soldier a clean handkerchief, and told him to hold it tightly against the wound in order to stop the bleeding.

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:08.320
The arm of another soldier was broken below the elbow.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:14.060
That corporal sure came through with flying colors.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:18.660
First, he sent a man for sticks to be used as splints.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:26.020
Then he gently removed the soldier's fatigue jacket, by tearing it down the back

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:36.960
and slipping it off the arm.

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:52.500
Meanwhile, the other soldier found several boards, and selected one which was most suitable.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:05.500
The corporal tore the victim's jacket into strips and used the larger pieces to pad the boards by wrapping the pieces around them.

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:11.920
This prevented injury to the skin and made the splints more comfortable.

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:34.820
The splints were then placed on the arm and tied in place with more strips of the jacket.

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:47.140
The ties were tight enough to hold the splint securely, but not so tight they cut off the circulation of blood.

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:55.260
They were placed above and below the fracture, but not over it.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:06.920
A sling was made with a longer strip of the torn jacket, long enough to keep the elbow at a right angle.

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:12.660
And that's what is known as using your first-aid head.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:23.000
This member of a Signal Corps wire-laying crew was up in a tree stringing a wire across a road.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:30.260
On the way down, his foot slipped and he fell to the ground.

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:41.200
The sergeant in charge of the detail examined his leg and found the left thigh bone was broken.

00:06:42.100 --> 00:06:45.426
So, he prepared padded splints from strips of shirt and boards.

00:06:45.426 --> 00:06:46.700
These splints had to be long enough to reach from the foot to the armpit on the outside and from the foot to the crotch on the inside.

00:06:46.700 --> 00:07:00.280
Ties were made above and below the fracture and around the body.

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:32.600
During the night, a storm had swept over the camp.

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:36.700
[ Thunder ]

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:42.780
It had blown down trees, branches, and a live wire.

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:49.592
Ryan had been through storms before, so he wasn't concerned.

00:07:49.592 --> 00:07:51.525
Didn't even see the wire.

00:07:51.526 --> 00:07:54.026
His foot caught on it, and the electricity knocked him unconscious.

00:07:54.026 --> 00:07:58.420
It was fortunate that he was discovered right away by two men who knew what to do.

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:06.120
First, they had to get Ryan off the wire, and second, as he was not breathing, they had to give him artificial respiration immediately.

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:10.659
In getting him off the wire, however, it was important not to touch him.

00:08:10.659 --> 00:08:13.625
They had to use something that wouldn't conduct electricity.

00:08:13.625 --> 00:08:16.559
In Ryan's case, one man went to get a dry pole.

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:19.400
The other man took off his GI belt.

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:23.080
Each man stood on his dry field jacket to insulate himself.

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:26.460
The belt strap was then hooked onto Ryan's feet

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:36.440
while the wire was removed with a broomstick.

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:50.200
As soon as he was moved a safe distance from the wire, he was given artificial respiration.

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:52.180
Speed was important.

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:08.759
One of the soldiers made sure Ryan didn't have anything loose in his mouth,

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:11.792
such as gum or false teeth that might catch in his throat,

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:14.720
and his tongue was pulled forward, so he wouldn't choke on it.

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:18.460
One arm was bent at the elbow, so that his head could rest on his hand.

00:09:18.900 --> 00:09:20.620
The other arm was put over his head.

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:25.259
Meanwhile the other soldier had knelt across his legs, placed his palms on the small of Ryan's back,

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:28.080
so that the little finger just touched his lowest rib.

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:32.860
Then, with arms straight, he swung forward slowly so that he leaned gradually on Ryan,

00:09:32.900 --> 00:09:36.592
then he moved back quickly to remove pressure suddenly and completely.

00:09:36.592 --> 00:09:38.025
But kept his hands in place.

00:09:38.025 --> 00:09:44.760
He kept doing this 12 to 15 times a minute to the cadence of out goes the bad air, in comes the good air.

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:50.200
Out goes the bad air, in comes the good air.

00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:55.660
Out goes the bad air, in comes the good air.

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:01.540
Out goes the bad air, in comes the good air.

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:05.592
After a while he got pretty tired, so the other soldier took over.

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:07.820
Being sure to keep the same cadence.

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:10.900
Out goes the bad air, in comes the good air.

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:16.120
Out goes the bad air, in comes the good air.

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:21.760
Out goes the bad air, in comes the good air.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:26.620
Out goes the bad air, in comes the good air.

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:31.160
For over an hour these men kept up artificial respiration,

00:10:31.700 --> 00:10:34.920
one man massaging the arms to help keep up the circulation.

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:43.260
Ryan finally started to breathe by himself and regained consciousness.

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:47.000
They watched him closely to make sure he did not stop breathing.

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:56.760
Afterward, they wrapped him up in blankets to keep him warm,

00:10:57.440 --> 00:10:59.060
and made him lie quiet.

00:11:00.260 --> 00:11:03.720
Hot tea or coffee was just the thing for Ryan at that time.

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:09.480
And as soon as they could, they got an ambulance and Ryan was taken to the hospital.

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:15.660
The same artificial respiration given Ryan, would be given to a man rescued from drowning.

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:18.640
Only one thing extra must be remembered.

00:11:19.160 --> 00:11:24.520
The moment the victim is on dry land he is laid facedown, and his hips are raised about 10 inches off

00:11:24.520 --> 00:11:27.700
the ground to let the water drain out of his lungs.

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:37.560
Then, he is placed face down, so that his head and shoulders are lower than the rest of his body, if possible.

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:42.440
This helps the water drain out while someone is working on him.

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:51.640
Now, let's see what happened to a cook in a field kitchen.

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:55.800
It was noon, and the men were preparing the mid-day meal.

00:11:57.340 --> 00:12:03.620
The cook had had plenty of experience in a kitchen, but maybe it just wasn't Cook's day.

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:31.892
Suddenly, the pot slipped and the hot contents came pouring down over his legs.

00:12:32.900 --> 00:12:35.620
Men were sent for medical help and first aid equipment.

00:12:36.980 --> 00:12:38.492
It must have hurt plenty.

00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:42.360
He was burned so badly, they couldn't pull his trousers off.

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:44.760
So, they cut them off,

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:49.720
laying aside the pieces carefully.

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:55.100
They got some cooling burn ointment out of the first-aid kit from the kitchen truck.

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:22.360
The cook's skin was badly blistered and the men knew enough not to break those blisters.

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:25.460
If they had, they might've caused infection.

00:13:26.700 --> 00:13:29.660
They gently spread the ointment on with the wooden applicator.

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:47.320
While the ointment was being applied, another man brought the cook's canteen and first-aid packet.

00:13:54.040 --> 00:13:55.600
He got out the wound tablets.

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:10.700
And gave him all of them.

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:18.540
He gave him plenty of water to wash them down.

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:25.100
If there hadn't been plenty of water for him to drink, he wouldn't have given him the tablets.

00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:44.980
Since the burn was bad, the men were afraid the cook would go into shock.

00:14:47.460 --> 00:15:02.080
So, they laid him on his back and raised his hips and feet so that his head was down.

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:22.440
They arranged boxes and 5-gallon cans around him.

00:15:24.900 --> 00:15:31.060
So that when a blanket was spread over him to keep him warm, it would not touch the burn.

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:44.760
They reassured the cook and told him that medical help was on the way.

00:15:50.980 --> 00:15:59.020
Minor dangers can all be treated by first-aid. Heat exhaustion, poison ivy, bellyache, foreign particles

00:15:59.020 --> 00:16:04.080
in the eye, ear, and nose, and splinters.

00:16:10.860 --> 00:16:19.140
Whatever is used to remove the splinter, a pin or a needle, or a pocketknife, it must first be sterilized with a flame.

00:16:32.880 --> 00:16:38.420
A small splinter may cause a serious infection if not removed promptly and properly.

00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:55.280
Now, a dab of iodine from a first-aid kit.

00:17:10.520 --> 00:17:16.820
The glass container is crushed with the fingers to release the iodine into the swab end.

00:17:33.180 --> 00:17:35.240
Then, a bandage to keep it clean,

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:39.140
being careful not to touch the inside of the dressing.

00:17:49.820 --> 00:17:51.780
These men were on a long hike.

00:17:52.780 --> 00:17:54.840
It was summer and plenty hot.

00:18:08.780 --> 00:18:11.580
A private almost passed out from heat exhaustion.

00:18:18.420 --> 00:18:20.860
He was taken to a cool, shady place.

00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:29.160
Clothes were loosened.

00:18:32.900 --> 00:18:34.820
And his field equipment removed.

00:18:55.260 --> 00:19:00.600
Salt water was prepared by dissolving two salt tablets in a canteen of water.

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:11.720
Then, they made the private drink plenty of the cool saltwater.

00:19:14.720 --> 00:19:22.580
Saltwater is the treatment for heat exhaustion and will also prevent heat exhaustion if taken before exposure to heat.

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:32.560
Wooded country such as this is a good breeding place for ticks.

00:19:34.160 --> 00:19:35.880
This man's got one on his leg.

00:19:36.440 --> 00:19:42.120
This animated drawing shows how the tick buried his head in the soldier's skin and started to swell.

00:19:42.800 --> 00:19:44.080
Don't try to pull it out.

00:19:44.300 --> 00:19:48.792
These hooks would hold the ticks head in the skin and only the body would come off.

00:19:48.792 --> 00:19:51.125
And don't crush the tick in your fingers.

00:19:51.125 --> 00:19:54.559
Ticks carry fever germs, and you might get infected.

00:19:55.380 --> 00:19:57.740
But it's a cinch to remove a tick properly.

00:19:58.500 --> 00:20:05.920
Hold a gasoline or kerosene-soaked rag against it for a while, or just hold the burning end of a cigarette close to it.

00:20:12.020 --> 00:20:15.220
And the tick will back out by itself and fall off.

00:20:22.660 --> 00:20:27.280
If there is a first-aid kit handy, paint the tick bite with iodine.

00:20:50.320 --> 00:20:51.400
Poison ivy.

00:20:58.820 --> 00:21:00.720
This man's got it on his hands.

00:21:01.460 --> 00:21:08.720
So, he gave himself immediate first-aid by scrubbing long and hard with GI soap to get the poison ivy off.

00:21:10.220 --> 00:21:14.359
If a rash appears, don't scratch it, and don't scrub it.

00:21:15.160 --> 00:21:17.360
Let it alone and report to sick call.

00:21:18.640 --> 00:21:21.420
Poison oak and poison sumac get the same treatment.

00:21:26.940 --> 00:21:28.480
This could happen to you.

00:21:30.620 --> 00:21:32.440
A soldier got something in his eye.

00:21:33.160 --> 00:21:34.300
Here's what to do.

00:21:34.660 --> 00:21:36.760
Close your eye and let some tears gather.

00:21:37.120 --> 00:21:39.760
If you're lucky, the particle will get washed out.

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:42.426
Whatever you do, don't rub the eye.

00:21:42.426 --> 00:21:44.220
Rubbing only makes it worse.

00:21:44.540 --> 00:21:48.460
If the tears don't wash it out, it may be stuck on the lower lid.

00:21:49.940 --> 00:21:56.220
Look up and get someone to pull the lid down and brush the object out with the corner of a clean handkerchief.

00:22:06.160 --> 00:22:09.480
To look at the upper lid, roll it up over a matchstick.

00:22:12.540 --> 00:22:15.780
At the same time, push the lid down gently with the match.

00:22:26.360 --> 00:22:32.720
If you can't find the speck easily, or if you should get something sharp in your eye, like a piece of glass or metal,

00:22:33.480 --> 00:22:39.240
do one thing and one thing only. See the medical officer.

00:22:44.140 --> 00:22:45.440
A bug in his ear!

00:22:51.280 --> 00:22:55.820
But did he pick it out with something sharp, like a pin? No, sir.

00:22:56.440 --> 00:22:58.120
That might've broken his eardrum.

00:23:01.900 --> 00:23:06.600
The soldier who was with him dropped some water in the ear and drowned that bug.

00:23:07.560 --> 00:23:13.160
If the foreign body is some object which may swell when wet, do not put water in the ear.

00:23:14.040 --> 00:23:15.520
See a medical officer.

00:23:21.920 --> 00:23:26.120
And by the way, don't go digging in your nose if you get something in it.

00:23:27.220 --> 00:23:29.560
A gentle blow into your handkerchief will get it out.

00:23:30.620 --> 00:23:34.440
And if something gets stuck in your throat try to hook it out with your finger.

00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:37.740
If you can't, try coughing it out.

00:23:54.340 --> 00:23:55.160
A bellyache.

00:24:01.880 --> 00:24:05.660
This man hadn't felt the call of nature for a couple of days.

00:24:08.220 --> 00:24:13.080
So, he figured he'd take a laxative, thinking it would relieve his pain.

00:24:23.260 --> 00:24:25.060
But a friend of his stopped him.

00:24:26.180 --> 00:24:33.200
He knew a pain in the stomach could be appendicitis and a laxative was the worst thing to take in such a case.

00:24:34.040 --> 00:24:37.580
His friend told him to lie down, while he went for the medical officer.

00:24:43.540 --> 00:24:50.560
His friend was right, that bellyache meant appendicitis, and called for hospital care.

00:24:55.500 --> 00:24:58.620
From where you're sitting this snake is pretty harmless.

00:24:59.000 --> 00:25:04.640
But it wasn't so harmless to these soldiers who were off on a reconnaissance patrol problem 
sketching the terrain.

00:25:12.960 --> 00:25:16.560
The corporal was bitten on the hand, and he was badly frightened.

00:25:17.500 --> 00:25:19.960
The soldiers started giving first-aid right away.

00:25:21.900 --> 00:25:28.980
First, they made a tourniquet with a handkerchief, tying it around the upper arm...

00:25:34.820 --> 00:25:38.140
and tightened it by twisting it with a stick.

00:25:46.260 --> 00:25:49.560
This kept the poison from spreading to the corporal's body.

00:25:50.700 --> 00:25:55.220
They sterilized the point of a knife by holding it in a match flame.

00:25:58.480 --> 00:26:09.560
Then, two crosscuts were made over each fang mark, one-quarter of an inch long, and deep enough to cause the blood to flow freely.

00:26:16.800 --> 00:26:21.760
No one had a suction cup, so a soldier sucked out the poison with his mouth.

00:26:22.560 --> 00:26:27.480
Something he could do safely, as long as his mouth was free from cuts or sores.

00:26:43.520 --> 00:26:48.380
The tourniquet had to be loosened for a few seconds at the end of every 20 minutes,

00:26:49.500 --> 00:26:55.900
so that some blood would circulate to the corporal's arm and prevent gangrene from developing.

00:27:05.660 --> 00:27:07.940
Meanwhile, the suction was continued.

00:27:21.900 --> 00:27:25.520
At the end of one hour, the tourniquet was removed.

00:27:41.720 --> 00:27:46.300
No small bandage was handy, so the wound was covered with a handkerchief.

00:27:47.820 --> 00:27:49.320
A clean handkerchief!

00:27:49.540 --> 00:28:02.140
[ Music ]

00:28:02.460 --> 00:28:09.500
In this way, practically all of the poison from the snake bite was removed and a soldier's life was saved.

00:28:13.100 --> 00:28:16.240
Remember these first-aid principles you have just seen.

00:28:17.380 --> 00:28:23.900
They may help you prevent serious, permanent disability, even save lives.

00:28:24.020 --> 00:28:25.520
Don't get panicky.

00:28:26.260 --> 00:28:28.759
Don't try to do too much.

00:28:29.080 --> 00:28:31.020
Know your first aid.

00:28:31.020 --> 00:28:32.360
And know it well.

00:28:32.360 --> 00:28:40.880
[Music]

